-- Props: mistakes="{[\"Overlooking Shadow as a Design Tool. Students often see shadow as an absence or problem. Ching treats shadow as a primary design element revealing form and organizing space. When analyzing an elevation or spatial plan, observe the shadows: what do they reveal? How do they create rhythm? Are they intended dramatic features or incidental? This analytical skill matters on the LEA.\", \"Not Recognizing Functional Texture Requirements. A polished floor looks elegant but is slippery and requires constant maintenance. A rough-textured floor provides grip and hides dirt but reads less formally. Texture choice involves functional considerations (acoustic absorption, safety, maintenance) as well as aesthetic ones. The LEA might ask how texture supports function: \\\"Why would a hospital choose smooth, cleanable surfaces over warm-textured wood?\\\"\", \"Forgetting Temporal Dimension. Light, shadow, and color all change over time. A space designed for noon sun feels different at dawn. The same building reads differently across seasons. Modern design increasingly embraces this temporal dimension—designing for how light moves, how views change, how seasons transform. The LEA tests whether you think dynamically about light and time, not just static appearance.\", \"Confusing Texture at Different Scleas. Texture operates at multiple scleas. A brick wall is course textured at human sclea; at architectural sclea it reads smooth (a unified plane of red). Concrete can be fine-textured (board-formed) or coarse (exposed aggregate) depending on finish. Don't assume texture is only tactile detail—large-sclea texture also operates visually and functionally.\", \"Overlooking Material Aging. Natural materials age visibly and beautifully (weathered wood, patinated copper, moss-covered stone). Some architects design specifically for aging and patina. Other materials (shiny metals, glass, smooth finishes) show wear obviously and require constant maintenance. The LEA tests whether you understand that finish choice is a long-term design decision affecting how a building ages.\"]}"
